# rules: go-waitgroup-add-inside-goroutine
# languages: go
# fixture: go/CWE-362.go
# from: CWE-362 (Race Condition), CWE-366
#
# wg.Add(1) written inside the goroutine races the wg.Wait() that is supposed
# to be waiting for it. The scheduler is under no obligation to start the
# goroutine before the calling function reaches Wait, so Wait can see a
# counter of zero, return immediately, and let the program carry on while the
# work it was waiting for has not started. It passes on a busy machine and
# fails on an idle one.
#
# Add belongs before the `go`, where being on the same goroutine as Wait is
# what orders it against Wait.
#
# The receiver's name is what tells a WaitGroup from an atomic counter, whose
# Add is spelled identically and whose correct place is exactly here, inside
# the goroutine. There is no other signal available: the matcher does not
# resolve types, and both are a selector call on a name.

["$W.Add($N)"] as $adds
| ["go func($$$_) { $$$GBODY }($$$_)"] as $goroutines
| scan_ast("*.go"; $adds + $goroutines) as $all
| ( $all | of($adds)
    | where_capture("W"; "(?i)^wg|waitgroup|group$")
    | within($all | of($goroutines)) )
| finding("go-waitgroup-add-inside-goroutine";
    "$W.Add runs on the new goroutine, so Wait can reach a counter of zero "
    + "and return before this work has started; count before the `go`")
| report
